✅ 21. Race Condition on a Shared Counter

21.1 Definition

A Race Condition occurs when:

🔴 Two or more threads access the same shared mutable resource.
🔴 At least one thread modifies it.
🔴 There is no proper synchronization.
🔴 The final result depends on the unpredictable execution order (race) of the threads.

In simple words:
🔴 Multiple threads are "racing" to modify the same data, leading to inconsistent or incorrect results.

21.2 Why Does a Race Condition Occur?

Suppose we have count = 0

Two threads execute
count++;

simultaneously. You might think

0
↓
1
↓
2

But that's not how it actually works.

21.3 count++ is NOT Atomic

This is one of the biggest interview points.
Most beginners think count++;

is a single operation.
❌ Wrong.

It actually consists of three operations.

Read count
↓
Increment
↓
Write count

Since these three steps can be interleaved between threads, race conditions occur.

21.4 Example Without Synchronization

class Counter {
    private int count = 0;
    public void increment() {
        count++;
    }

    public int getCount() {
        return count;
    }
}

Main Class

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Counter counter = new Counter();

        Thread t1 = new Thread(() -> {
            for(int i=0;i<100000;i++){
                counter.increment();
            }
        });

        Thread t2 = new Thread(() -> {
            for(int i=0;i<100000;i++){
                counter.increment();
            }
        });

        t1.start();
        t2.start();
        t1.join();
        t2.join();
        System.out.println(counter.getCount());

    }
}

21.5 Expected Output

We expect 200000
because 100000 + 100000 = 200000

21.6 Actual Output

Possible outputs: 198756 or 199321 or 197842

Every execution may produce a different answer.
This is the definition of a race condition.

21.7 Step-by-Step Internal Working

Suppose count = 5

Both threads execute
count++;
at the same time.

Thread-A

Read count
↓
5

Thread-B

Before Thread-A writes back,
it also executes

Read count
↓
5

Now

Both threads think
count = 5

Thread-A

5 + 1
↓
6

Writes
count = 6

Thread-B

Still has its own copy

5 + 1
↓
6

Writes
count = 6

Final Value
Instead of 7

we get
6
One increment is lost.
This is called a Lost Update.

